     well as the content of mobile forms of heavy metals: lead, cadmium, copper, zinc, mercury, cobalt, and molybdenum;      boron, and radioactive caesium were determined in the soil after sunflower cultivation. The factual content of soil      contaminants was compared with their maximum permissible concentrations. The study investigated the content      of heavy metals, namely, lead, cadmium, copper, and zinc, as well as nitrates in sunflower seeds. Their content was      compared with the values of maximum permissible concentrations. The coefficients of accumulation of the heavy      metals under study by sunflower seeds were calculated. Thus, copper and zinc are most intensively absorbed from      the soil and accumulate in sunflower seeds, while lead is the least absorbed. The findings suggest that since no      excess of the permissible levels of heavy metals and nitrates in sunflower seeds was found, there is a higher      probability of their increased accumulation in sunflower by-products: seed husks, stems, leaves, petioles, and roots. 
