 conducted to determine six heavy metals; lead, cadmium, chromium, copper, zinc and iron in thirty-six composite  sunflower seeds collected from different ecological zones of Sindh. Metals were determined after wet acid digestion by  using flame atomic absorption spectroscopy (FAAS). Individual mean concentrations were found in the range of 0.006–  0.014 mg/kg (Cd), 0.038–0.063 mg/kg (Pb), 0.11–0.19 mg/kg (Cr), 2.44–3.74 mg/kg (Cu), 14.32–20.15 mg/kg (Zn), and  36.1–47.3 mg/kg (Fe) on a dry weight basis. All metal concentrations remained well within FAO/WHO permissible limits;  however, samples collected from Tando jam and Kotri had comparatively higher levels due anthropogenic influence on  this ecological zone.  Keywords: Sunflower seeds, Heavy metals, Edible oil, Food safety, Pakistan. 

